\n- Item base premiums: 100 (sword) + 60 (amulet) = 160\n- Policy base: 160\n- Item-specific (curse on sword): +50\n- Policy base premium for policy-wide modifiers: 160? or 210?\n- \"policy base premium 160 G; the cursed surcharge adds 50 G\"\n- \"→ 210 G before further modifiers and fee\"\n\nSo the cursed surcharge is 50% of the cursed sword's base premium (100 * 0.5 = 50), added to the policy total.\n\nIf \"policy base premium\" used for policy-wide modifiers is 160 (sum of base premiums), and item-specific surcharges are added on top... then 210 = 160 + 50. The spec says \"210 G before further modifiers and fee\" — so this is after item-specific but before policy-wide.\n\nOK let me model it as:\n- itemBasePremium(item) = base from price list (with block rules)\n- itemAdjustment(item) = base + cursed surcharge + high enchant surcharge\n- policyBaseSum = sum of itemBasePremium\n- itemSurcharges = sum of (item-specific surcharges per item)\n- policySubtotal = policyBaseSum + itemSurcharges\n\nFor policy-wide modifiers:\n- The spec is a bit unclear. Let me look at what makes the long-standing example work.\n\nIf policy-wide modifiers apply to itemBasePremium (the \"sum of all item base premiums\"):\n- Long-standing example: 100 (sword base)\n- Loyalty: -20% of 100 = -20\n- First insurance: +10% of 100 = +10\n- Follow-up: -15% of 100 = -15\n\nIf policy-wide modifiers apply to policySubtotal (after item-specific surcharges):\n- Long-standing example: 180 (after item-specific)\n- Loyalty: -20% of 180 = -36\n- First insurance: +10% of 180 = +18\n- Follow-up: -15% of 180 = -27\n- Result: 180 - 36 + 18 - 27 = 135, +5 fee = 140. Not 160.\n\nSo clearly policy-wide modifiers apply to the sum of itemBasePremium (100 in this case).\n\nFor the cursed sword + amulet example, the spec says \"policy base premium 160 G\" - so before cursed surcharge. And 210 G after cursed surcharge (which is item-specific). So policy-wide modifiers would apply to 160 (policy base) or 210 (after item-specific)?\n\nThe spec just says \"210 G before further modifiers and fee\" - which is after item-specific, before policy-wide. If loyalty applied, it'd apply to... 160? 210? \n\nIn the long-standing example, the policy base was 100 (one sword) and after item-specific surcharges was 180. The loyalty discount was 20, which is 20% of 100. So loyalty is 20% of the sum of base premiums (i.e., \"policy base\" before item-specific).\n\nOK so my model:\n- itemBasePremium(item): from price list\n- policyBaseSum = sum of itemBasePremium (this is what policy-wide modifiers apply to)\n- itemSurchargesSum = sum of (item-specific surcharges: curse, high enchant)\n- policySubtotal = policyBaseSum + itemSurchargesSum\n- For policy-wide modifiers:\n  - Each modifier is applied to policyBaseSum\n  - Loyalty discount: -20% of policyBaseSum if yearsWithMHPCO ≥ 2\n  - First insurance: +10% of policyBaseSum per item? Or +10% of policyBaseSum once?\n  - Follow-up contract: -15% of policyBaseSum if it's not the first quote\n\nHmm, but the spec says \"each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance\". This suggests first insurance applies per-item. But in the long-standing example with one item, it's +10. So first insurance is per-item, but in that example there's only one item, so it's just +10.\n\nOK so first insurance is per-item, applied to each item's base premium, summed up.\n\nLet me check: if there were two swords, first insurance would be +10 per sword = +20 (10% of 100, twice).\n\nActually let me re-read: \"Customers receive a 15 % discount on each contract after their first.\"\n\nSo follow-up contract applies to the policy (each quote after the first one for that customer gets the discount).\n\n\"A first insurance carries a 10 % initial assessment surcharge.\"\n\nSo first insurance applies to each item being insured for the first time. In a quote, every item is a first insurance. So each item in a quote gets +10% of its base premium as a surcharge.\n\nLet me verify with the long-standing example:\n- Sword base: 100\n- Per-item first insurance: +10 (10% of 100)\n- Total first insurance: 10 (since there's only one item)\n\n100 + 50 + 30 = 180 (after item-specific)\n- 20 loyalty (20% of 100 policy base)\n- + 10 first insurance (10% of 100, per item, only one item)\n- - 15 follow-up (15% of 100 policy base)\n= 180 - 20 + 10 - 15 = 155 ✓\n+ 5 fee = 160 ✓\n\nAnd the newcomer example:\n- Sword base: 100, not yet a customer (0 years, no previous contract)\n- First contract (no follow-up discount)\n- Per-item first insurance: +10\n\n100 + 50 + 10 = 160 (item base + curse + first insurance)\n- No loyalty (0 years)\n- No follow-up discount (first contract)\n= 160\n+ 5 fee = 165 ✓\n\nOK that all checks out.\n\nNow what about the \"first insurance\" surcharge being per-item? The spec says \"each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\"\n\nSo I think the 10% is applied to each item's base premium. Each item gets +10% on its own base.\n\nBut wait, in the long-standing example, the math is: +10 first insurance. If this is 10% of sword base (100), that's 10. ✓\n\nIn a multi-item scenario (like the cursed sword + plain amulet example), if first insurance is 10% per item:\n- Sword: 10\n- Amulet: 6\n- Total: 16\n\nIf first insurance is 10% of policy base (160), that's 16. Same answer!\n\nFor a 2-sword policy, per-item would be 10 + 10 = 20, while per-policy would be 20. Same again.\n\nSo for this kata, it doesn't really matter how we model it, as long as we get the right answer.\n\nLet me now also think about cap and insurance:\n- \"Insurance sum\" = sum of insurance values for all items in policy\n- \"Cap\" = 2 × insurance sum\n- \"Payout\" per claim ≤ remaining cap\n\nInsurance values:\n- Sword: 1000\n- Amulet: 600\n- Staff: 800\n- Potion: 400\n- Component: 250 each\n\nThe block discount doesn't affect insurance sum (per spec).\n\nCap exhaustion example:\n- Sword + 3 runes → insurance sum = 1000 + 3×250 = 1750 (correct, block doesn't change insurance)\n- Cap = 3500\n- The cursed sword with premium 165 G: insurance value is 1000, cap 2000 (based on unmodified insurance)\n\nOK I think I have enough understanding. Let me write the test list.\n\nActually wait, let me also think about how claim processing works:\n\nClaim processing:\n1. Per damage event: 100 G deductible\n2. Cap per policy: 2× insurance sum, decreases after each claim\n\nPer damage item:\n- If item has enchantment ≥ 8: 50% reimbursement\n- Else if item made of dragon material: full reimbursement\n- Otherwise: full reimbursement\n- Then apply 100 G deductible\n\nWait, the dragon material vs high enchantment logic. From the examples:\n- Dragon-material sword, enchant 9, damage 1000 → payout 400 (both clauses apply; the 50% rule wins, then deductible: 500-100)\n- Dragon-material sword, enchant 5, damage 800 → payout 700 (only dragon material applies: full reimbursement, then deductible: 800-100)\n- Steel sword, enchant 9, damage 1000 → payout 400 (only high-enchantment: 50%, then deductible)\n\nSo:\n- enchantment ≥ 8 AND dragon material → 50% applies (high enchantment wins)\n- enchantment ≥ 8 only → 50%\n- dragon material only → 100%\n- neither → 100%\n\nWait, enchant 9 is ≥ 8 so high enchantment clause applies. For dragon material with enchant 9, both could apply. The spec says \"the 50 % rule wins\". So if both, use 50%.\n\nFor steel sword with enchant 9, only high enchantment applies: 50% → 500, then deductible -100 = 400.\n\nFor dragon-material sword with enchant 8 (≥ 8) damage 1000 → 50% wins, then deductible → 500-100 = 400. ✓\n\nFor dragon-material sword with enchant 5 damage 800 → dragon material only: full, then deductible → 800-100 = 700. ✓\n\nOK so the logic is:\n- If enchantment ≥ 8: 50% reimbursement, THEN deductible\n- Else if dragon material: 100% reimbursement, THEN deductible\n- Else: 100% reimbursement, THEN deductible\n\nThe 100% cases differ only in WHICH clause applies. The math is the same.\n\nActually wait, re-reading more carefully:\n\"Damage to items with enchantment level ≥ 8 is reimbursed at 50 % of the damage amount.\"\n\"Damage to items made of dragon material is fully reimbursed.\"\n\nSo these are mutually exclusive? Let me check the dragon-material sword with enchant 9 example: \"both clauses apply; the 50 % rule wins\".\n\nOK so they can both apply. The high enchantment clause takes priority when both apply.\n\nImplementation:\n```\nif (enchantment >= 8) {\n    reimburse = amount * 0.5\n} else if (dragonMaterial) {\n    reimburse = amount\n} else {\n    reimburse = amount\n}\nreimburse -= 100 (deductible)\nreimburse = floor(reimburse)  // round down\n```\n\nWait, \"in MHPCO's favor\" — for premiums we round UP, for payouts we round DOWN.\n\nFor the dragon-material sword with enchant 8 damage 1000:\n- 1000 * 0.5 = 500\n- 500 - 100 = 400 ✓\n\nFor the regular sword (steel, enchant 3) damage 500:\n- 500 - 100 = 400 ✓\n\nFor the rune damage 200:\n- 200 - 100 = 100 ✓\n\nFor the dragon attack on sword 500 + amulet 300:\n- Sword: 500 - 100 = 400\n- Amulet: 300 - 100 = 200\n- Total: 600 ✓\n\nFor the cap exhaustion, two successive claims of 1500 G:\n- First: 1500 - 100 = 1400, cap remaining = 2000 - 1400 = 600 ✓\n- Second: desired 1500 - 100 = 1400, but cap remaining is 600, so payout = 600, cap remaining = 0 ✓\n\nOK my understanding is solid. Now let me think about the test file structure.\n\nI'll have a `processScenario` function that takes the parsed JSON and returns the results. For error cases, it will throw. The CLI will catch and exit non-zero.\n\nLet me now think about all the test cases I want. I'll be comprehensive.\n\nLet me write the test list:\n\n```typescript\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { processScenario } from \"./claim-office.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"MHPCO Claim Office\", () => {\n  // === Empty / minimal cases ===\n  it.todo(\"quote with empty item list returns premium of 5 G (only processing fee)\");\n  \n  // === Single main item, no modifiers ===\n  it.todo(\"quote for a single sword with no modifiers returns premium 105 G (100 base + 5 fee)\");\n  it.todo(\"quote for a single amulet with no modifiers returns premium 65 G (60 base + 5 fee)\");\n  it.todo(\"quote for a single staff with no modifiers returns premium 85 G (80 base + 5 fee)\");\n  it.todo(\"quote for a single potion with no modifiers returns premium 45 G (40 base + 5 fee)\");\n  \n  // === Components in isolation ===\n  it.todo(\"2 runes → 50 G base premium\");\n  it.todo(\"3 runes → 60 G base premium (block applies)\");\n  it.todo(\"4 runes → 100 G base premium (no block)\");\n  it.todo(\"7 runes → 175 G base premium\");\n  it.todo(\"2 runes + 1 moonstone → 75 G base premium (no block: different types)\");\n  it.todo(\"3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G base premium (two separate blocks)\");\n  \n  // === Item-specific modifiers ===\n  it.todo(\"cursed sword adds 50% risk surcharge (50 G on top of 100 base)\");\n  it.todo(\"sword with enchantment 5 adds 30% surcharge (30 G)\");\n  it.todo(\"sword with enchantment 4 does NOT add high-enchantment surcharge\");\n  it.todo(\"cursed sword with enchantment 5 adds both surcharges (50 + 30)\");\n  \n  // === Policy-wide modifiers ===\n  it.todo(\"customer with 2 years receives 20% loyalty discount\");\n  it.todo(\"customer with 3 years receives 20% loyalty discount (≥ 2 threshold)\");\n  it.todo(\"customer with 1 year does NOT receive loyalty discount\");\n  it.todo(\"first insurance surcharge applies (10% per item)\");\n  it.todo(\"follow-up contract (second quote) gives 15% discount\");\n  \n  // === Multi-item policies ===\n  it.todo(\"cursed sword + plain amulet: policy base 160 + cursed 50 = 210\");\n  it.todo(\"item-specific modifiers apply to affected item only\");\n  it.todo(\"policy-wide modifiers apply to sum of base premiums\");\n  \n  // === Rounding ===\n  it.todo(\"premium of 197.5 G rounds up to 198 G\");\n  it.todo(\"payout of 350.5 G rounds down to