 a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\"\n\nSo this is already covered by the integration example. Good.\n\nLet me also add tests for:\n- Quote input with optional fields (e.g., sword without material/enchantment/cursed)\n\nActually, I think I should also test that:\n- A quote step's \"items\" can have various shapes (with/without material, enchantment, cursed)\n- Materials other than \"dragon\" don't trigger dragon material clause\n\nLet me also think about what happens with negative enchantment. The spec doesn't say, so we shouldn't worry about it.\n\nOK let me write out a comprehensive test list. I'll keep it organized but thorough.\n\nActually, one more thing to verify - the \"first insurance\" being per item:\n\n\"The first insurance surcharge still applies to the new sword, even though the customer is on a follow-up contract — each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\"\n\nSo per item, +10% of that item's base premium. Let me verify with the long-standing example:\n- 1 cursed sword: 100 + 50 + 30 = 180 (after item-specific)\n- Per-item first insurance on sword: +10 (10% of 100)\n- - 20 loyalty (20% of 100, policy base)\n- - 15 follow-up (15% of 100, policy base)\n- 180 + 10 - 20 - 15 = 155 ✓\n- + 5 fee = 160 ✓\n\nOK so the test will verify: first insurance is +10% of each item's base premium (which for a single sword is +10).\n\nNow what if there are multiple items? E.g., 2 swords + 1 amulet:\n- Per-item first insurance: 10 + 10 + 6 = 26 (or just 10% of policy base = 100+100+60 = 260 * 0.1 = 26)\n\nSame answer either way.\n\nOK let me finalize the test list. I'll write it now.\n\nActually, I want to also include tests for the CLI behavior since the spec emphasizes that. Let me think about this:\n\nThe CLI behavior tests would:\n- Spawn the CLI as a subprocess\n- Provide stdin input\n- Verify stdout/stderr/exit code\n\nThis is more complex but more accurate to the spec. Let me include a few of these tests.\n\nFor the test list, I'll include:\n- Pure logic tests (most of them)\n- A few CLI integration tests\n\nOK let me write the test list now.\n\nLet me think about the imports. I need:\n- `processScenario` from `./claim-office.js`\n- Maybe `runCLI` or similar for CLI tests\n\nFor CLI tests, I can spawn a subprocess using Node's `child_process.spawnSync` or vitest's test framework. Let me use a simpler approach: have a `runCLI(input: string): {stdout, stderr, exitCode}` helper.\n\nOr even simpler: have `runCLI(input: string): {stdout, stderr, exitCode}` defined as a helper function inside the test file.\n\nActually, the simplest approach: my CLI logic is split:\n- `processScenario(scenario): ScenarioResults` — pure function\n- `runCLI(args)` — reads stdin, calls processScenario, writes stdout/stderr, sets exit code\n\nI can test `processScenario` directly for the logic. For the CLI error behavior (non-zero exit code, stderr), I need to test the CLI.\n\nLet me use `execa` or just Node's child_process. Since this is a TypeScript project with vitest, I'll use the simpler approach: spawn `tsx src/cli.ts` or `node dist/cli.js`.\n\nActually, for test purposes, let me define a helper:\n\n```typescript\nimport { spawnSync } from \"node:child_process\";\n\nfunction runCLI(input: string): { stdout: string; stderr: string; exitCode: number } {\n  const result = spawnSync(\"npx\", [\"tsx\", \"src/cli.ts\"], {\n    input,\n    encoding: \"utf-8\",\n  });\n  return {\n    stdout: result.stdout ?? \"\",\n    stderr: result.stderr ?? \"\",\n    exitCode: result.status ?? -1,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nThis should work for CLI tests.\n\nOK now let me think about my final test list structure. I'll have:\n\n1. Quote tests (premium calculation) - most tests\n2. Claim tests (payout calculation)\n3. Multi-step scenarios\n4. CLI behavior tests\n\nWithin quote tests:\n- Single item, no modifiers\n- Components, blocks\n- Item-specific modifiers (cursed, high enchant)\n- Policy-wide modifiers (loyalty, first insurance, follow-up)\n- Multi-item\n- Rounding\n- Empty list\n- Error: unknown type\n\nWithin claim tests:\n- Standard reimbursement (no special clauses)\n- High enchantment clause\n- Dragon material clause\n- Multiple damages (per-item deductible)\n- Cap exhaustion\n- Error: damage to item not in policy\n- Error: unknown item type\n- Error: negative amount\n\nMulti-step:\n- Quote then claim\n- Two quotes then two claims\n- Cap tracked across claims\n\nCLI:\n- Quote + claim happy path\n- Error case → non-zero exit + stderr message\n\nOK let me also think: for the \"item-specific modifiers\" example:\n\"a policy covers a cursed sword (base premium 100 G) and a plain amulet (base premium 60 G) → policy base premium 160 G; the cursed surcharge adds 50 G (50 % of the cursed sword's base premium, not of the policy total) → 210 G before further modifiers and fee\"\n\nThis is a multi-item policy with only an item-specific modifier and the processing fee. So:\n- Item base: 100 (sword) + 60 (amulet) = 160\n- Item-specific: +50 (cursed sword)\n- Policy-wide: 0 (no loyalty, no first insurance mentioned here, no follow-up)\n- Subtotal: 210\n- Fee: +5\n- Premium: 215\n\nWait, but the spec says \"210 G before further modifiers and fee\". So 210 is BEFORE first insurance, follow-up, loyalty, and the fee.\n\nIf first insurance applies (always, since each item is a first insurance):\n- Per-item first insurance: 10 (sword) + 6 (amulet) = 16\n- 210 + 16 = 226\n- + 5 fee = 231\n\nHmm but the spec just says 210 G before further modifiers and fee. I think the spec is just illustrating the BASE premium without going through all modifiers. The actual computation would include first insurance.\n\nOK I'll have a test that just verifies the \"policy base premium 160 G + cursed surcharge 50 G\" interpretation. Without further modifiers, it'd be 160 + 50 = 210 + 5 fee = 215 (for a customer with 0 years, first contract). But actually first insurance would still apply...\n\nOK let me not include this specific test - it's already covered by the integration examples.\n\nActually wait, the spec says: \"→ 210 G before further modifiers and fee\". So in this example, the premium calculation is incomplete because we don't have all the info. The test I write should reflect the FULL computation:\n\nFor \"customer with 0 years, no previous contract\" (newcomer):\n- Sword base 100, cursed +50, first insurance +10 (10% of 100)\n- Amulet base 60, first insurance +6 (10% of 60)\n- Policy base: 100 + 60 = 160\n- Item-specific surcharge: +50 (cursed sword)\n- Policy-wide (loyalty 0%, follow-up 0%): no change\n- First insurance surcharge: +16\n- Subtotal: 160 + 50 + 16 = 226\n- Fee: +5\n- Premium: 231\n\nSo I'll have a test for this scenario.\n\nFor \"customer with 2+ years, second contract\":\n- Sword base 100, cursed +50, first insurance +10\n- Amulet base 60, first insurance +6\n- Policy base: 160\n- Loyalty: -20% of 160 = -32\n- Follow-up: -15% of 160 = -24\n- Item-specific: +50\n- First insurance: +16\n- Subtotal: 160 + 50 + 16 - 32 - 24 = 170\n- Fee: +5\n- Premium: 175\n\nOK but these calculations aren't in the spec explicitly. Let me only test what the spec explicitly says.\n\nActually I realize I should be more careful with what to test. The spec gives specific numerical examples that I can test directly. For combinations not explicitly given, I should be careful.\n\nLet me focus on:\n1. The explicit numerical examples in the spec\n2. The integration examples\n3. Edge cases explicitly mentioned\n\nFor each, write a test with the expected value.\n\nLet me list them all:\n\n### Premium calculation examples:\n\n1. **Empty list**: empty items → premium 5 (only processing fee)\n2. **2 runes**: → 50 base premium → 55 with fee\n3. **3 runes**: → 60 base premium → 65 with fee (block applies)\n4. **4 runes**: → 100 base premium → 105 with fee\n5. **7 runes**: → 175 base premium → 180 with fee\n6. **2 runes + 1 moonstone**: → 75 base → 80 with fee\n7. **3 runes + 3 moonstones**: → 120 base → 125 with fee\n8. **Newcomer (0 yr) cursed sword**: → 165\n9. **Long-standing customer (3 yr) 2nd contract, cursed sword enchant 7**: → 160\n\nWait, examples 2-7 say \"base premium\" — but the final premium would also include the processing fee. Let me re-read...\n\n\"The examples below illustrate single rules in isolation. Values shown are base premiums or raw payout amounts before integration with other modifiers, unless stated otherwise.\"\n\nOK so 50, 60, 100, 175, 75, 120 are all base premiums before the processing fee. So:\n- 2 runes → 50 base → 55 premium (with fee)\n- 3 runes → 60 base → 65 premium\n- 4 runes → 100 base → 105 premium\n- 7 runes → 175 base → 180 premium\n- 2 runes + 1 moonstone → 75 base → 80 premium\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 base → 125 premium\n\nFor the integration examples, the value already includes the fee:\n- Newcomer cursed sword → 165 (with fee)\n- Long-standing customer → 160 (with fee)\n\nOK so my tests need to be careful about whether fee is included.\n\nLet me organize:\n\n**Premium tests (with processing fee unless noted):**\n1. Empty items → 5\n2. 2 runes → 55 (50 base + 5 fee)\n3. 3 runes → 65 (60 base + 5 fee)\n4. 4 runes → 105 (100 base + 5 fee)\n5. 7 runes → 180 (175 base + 5 fee)\n6. 2 runes + 1 moonstone → 80 (75 base + 5 fee)\n7. 3 runes + 3 moonstones → 125 (120 base + 5 fee)\n8. Sword (no modifiers) → 105 (100 base + 5 fee)\n9. Sword (cursed) → 155 (100 + 50 + 5 fee)\n10. Sword (enchantment 5) → 135 (100 + 30 + 5 fee)\n11. Sword (cursed, enchant 5) → 185 (100 + 50 + 30 + 5 fee)\n12. Sword (enchantment 4) → 105 (no high enchant surcharge)\n13. Newcomer (0 yr) cursed sword → 165\n14. Long-standing (3 yr) 2nd contract, cursed sword enchant 7 → 160\n15. Cursed sword + plain amulet → 231 (160 + 50 + 16 + 5 fee)? Or is this too much?\n\nHmm. Let me skip 15 because the spec doesn't give a definitive answer.\n\nWait actually the spec says: \"210 G before further modifiers and fee\". So 210 is before everything except item-specific (curse) and fee. So first insurance WOULD apply. The spec might be implying that in the simple example, the first insurance is omitted.\n\nLet me re-read the spec example:\n\"a policy covers a cursed sword (base premium 100 G) and a plain amulet (base premium 60 G) → policy base premium 160 G; the cursed surcharge adds 50 G (50 % of the cursed sword's base premium, not of the policy total) → 210 G before further modifiers and fee\"\n\nOK so this example demonstrates that the cursed surcharge is 50% of the CURSED ITEM's base premium (100 * 0.5 = 50), not 50% of the policy total (160 * 0.5 = 80). That's the educational point.\n\nThe \"before further modifiers and fee\" suggests there could be more modifiers (first insurance, loyalty, follow-up) but they're not applied here because the example is just demonstrating the cursed surcharge scope.\n\nOK so I think the spec is showing partial computations to illustrate concepts. The full computation would also include first insurance (always) and possibly loyalty/follow-up.\n\nI'll skip this specific test since it's not a complete example.\n\nNow let me also think about what tests to add for claim processing:\n\n**Claim tests:**\n1. Regular sword (steel, enchant 3), damage 500 → payout 400 (cap = 2000)\n2. Rune damage 200 → payout 100 (cap = 500, insurance value 250)\n3. Dragon-material sword, enchant 8, damage 1000 → payout 400 (cap = 2000)\n4. Dragon-material sword, enchant 5, damage 800 → payout 700\n5. Steel sword, enchant 9, damage 1000 → payout 400\n6. Dragon attack: sword 500 + amulet 300 → payout 600\n7. Two swords in policy → insurance sum 2000, cap 4000\n8. Two sword damages → each gets its own deductible\n9. Cap exhaustion: sword cap 2000, first claim 1400, second claim 600 (after 1400 first)\n\nWait, for #9 the spec says \"first claim → payout 1400 G, cap remaining 600 G; second claim → payout 600 G, cap remaining 0 G\". So each claim has damage 1500, no special clauses, regular sword.\n\nFor two claims of 1500 each:\n- First: 1500 - 100 = 1400, remaining cap = 2000 - 1400 = 600\n- Second: 1500 - 100 = 1400 desired, but cap remaining = 600, so payout = 600, remaining cap = 0\n\n**Errors:**\n1. Quote with unknown item type → error\n2. Claim with damage to item not in policy → error\n3. Claim with damage to unknown item type → error\n4. Claim with negative damage amount → error\n\n**CLI:**\n1. Happy path: quote + claim → JSON results to stdout\n2. Error: stderr message, non-zero exit\n\n**Rounding:**\n1. Premium of 197.5 → 198\n2. Payout of 350.5 → 350\n\nBut I need to construct scenarios that produce these specific fractional values. Let me think...\n\nFor premium of 197.5 → 198 (rounded up):\n- Need something that produces 197.5 before fee\n- Fee is 5, so premium is computed_amount + 5 = 197.5? No, 197.5 is after rounding. So pre-rounding premium is some fraction.\n- Hmm, the spec says \"intermediate amounts during a calculation are kept as fractions; only the final premium or payout is rounded\". So the premium starts as some value, + fee = some value, and we round that.\n- 197.5 after fee means pre-fee was 192.5. What gives 192.5?\n- Actually no. The fee is 5 G added. So premium = computed_premium + 5 = 197.5 → computed_premium = 192.5.\n- Hmm I need to think of a scenario that gives 192.5.\n\nActually, the rounding example is just illustrative. I don't need to construct a scenario for it - I can write a unit test directly on a rounding function if I structure my code that way.\n\nOr I can skip the explicit rounding test in the test list and let the integration tests cover it.\n\nLet me skip explicit rounding tests for now. The integration tests will likely use whole numbers anyway.\n\nOK let me finalize the test list now.\n\nActually, one more consideration. The spec mentions:\n\"if the `damages` array contains more entries of a given type than the policy actually covers (e.g. two sword damages but only one sword insured) → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code; the whole claim is rejected\"\n\nSo this is an error case I need to test.\n\nLet me finalize my test list. I'll organize it into describes for clarity.\n\n```typescript\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { processScenario } from \"./claim-office.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"MHPCO Claim Office\", () => {\n  describe(\"Quote - empty and basic items\", () => {\n    it.todo(\"empty items list returns premium 5 G (only processing fee)\");\n    it.todo(\"a sword with no modifiers returns premium 105 G\");\n    it.todo(\"an amulet with no modifiers returns premium 65 G\");\n    it.todo(\"a staff with no modifiers returns premium 85 G\");\n    it.todo(\"a potion with no modifiers returns premium 45 G\");\n  });\n\n  describe(\"Quote - components and building blocks\", () => {\n    it.todo(\"2 runes → 55 G (50 base + 5 fee)\");\n    it.todo(\"3 runes → 65 G (60 base + 5 fee, block applies)\");\n    it.todo(\"4 runes → 105 G (100 base + 5 fee, no block)\");\n    it.todo(\"7 runes → 180 G (175 base + 5 fee)\");\n    it.todo(\"2 runes + 1 moonstone → 80 G (75 base + 5 fee, no block)\");\n    it.todo(\"3 runes + 3 moonstones → 125 G (120 base + 5 fee, two blocks)\");\n  });\n\n  describe(\"Quote - item-specific modifiers\", () => {\n    it.todo(\"cursed sword adds 50% risk surcharge (premium 155 G)\");\n    it.todo(\"sword with enchantment 5 adds 30% surcharge (premium 135 G)\");\n    it.todo(\"sword with enchantment 4 does NOT add high-enchantment surcharge\");\n    it.todo(\"cursed sword with enchantment 5 adds both surcharges\");\n  });\n\n  describe(\"Quote - policy-wide modifiers\", () => {\n    it.todo(\"customer with 2 years receives 20% loyalty discount\");\n    it.todo(\"customer with 3 years receives 20% loyalty discount\");\n    it.todo(\"customer with 1 year does NOT receive loyalty discount\");\n    it.todo(\"first insurance applies to each item (10% of item base premium)\");\n    it.todo(\"follow-up