s to stderr and exits non-zero\");\n  });\n});\n```\n\nThat's a comprehensive test list. Let me also think about whether I missed anything from the spec:\n\n- \"first insurance applies per item\" - covered by integration test\n- \"cursed surcharge applies to affected item only\" - the spec says this but doesn't give a concrete premium number for multi-item. I'll cover it through the integration tests.\n- \"policy-wide modifiers apply to policy base (sum of item base premiums)\" - covered\n- \"processing fee added at end\" - covered\n- \"rounding to whole G in MHPCO's favor\" - covered\n- \"empty item list\" - covered\n- \"unknown type\" - covered\n- \"claim to item not in policy\" - covered\n- \"negative damage amount\" - covered\n\nOK I think the test list is comprehensive. Let me write it now.\n\nActually, I just realized: I should also test the multi-step scenario explicitly. The spec mentions:\n\"Steps are processed sequentially; later claim steps refer to a policy created by an earlier quote step via its zero-based step index in a policy field.\"\n\nSo a multi-step scenario with quote + claim should work. Let me add a test for that.\n\nAlso, I want to make sure I test the \"remainingCap\" in claim results, not just \"payout\".\n\nLet me finalize and write.\n\nWait, let me reconsider the structure of the code I'm going to write.\n\nThe CLI reads JSON from stdin. So the input is JSON. I'll have:\n- `processScenario(scenario)` — pure function\n- `runCLI()` — reads stdin, calls processScenario, writes stdout, exits\n\nFor testing processScenario, I can pass it parsed objects.\n\nLet me also think about how to handle the `policy` index in claims. When processing step i, if it's a claim with policy=j, I need to look up the policy created at step j. Since steps are sequential, all quotes up to i-1 have been processed. So j < i.\n\nI'll store policies in an array indexed by their step number. A quote at step i creates a policy at index i.\n\nLet me also think about what happens if a claim references a step that's not a quote, or doesn't exist. The spec doesn't say, so I'll throw an error.\n\nOK let me write the code.\n\nActually, let me think about the test for \"policy with two swords\". I need to specify that the policy covers two swords. So in my test input:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"customer\": {\"yearsWithMHPCO\": 0},\n  \"steps\": [\n    {\n      \"op\": \"quote\",\n      \"items\": [\n        {\"type\": \"sword\"},\n        {\"type\": \"sword\"}\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"op\": \"claim\",\n      \"policy\": 0,\n      \"incident\": {\n        \"cause\": \"test\",\n        \"damages\": [\n          {\"itemType\": \"sword\", \"amount\": 500},\n          {\"itemType\": \"sword\", \"amount\": 500}\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nExpected: each sword damage has its own deductible. Two damages of 500 each, each gets 100 deductible → 800 payout. Insurance sum 2000, cap 4000. Remaining cap 3200.\n\nHmm wait, the spec says \"if the damages array contains more entries of a given type than the policy actually covers (e.g. two sword damages but only one sword insured) → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code\". So the example explicitly mentions \"two sword damages but only one sword insured\" as the error case. But what if the policy has two swords and damages has two entries of sword? That's fine - each sword gets damaged.\n\nOK so my test for \"two sword damages\" should be:\n- Policy: 2 swords\n- Damages: 2 swords at 500 each\n- Expected: payout 800 (500-100 + 500-100), remaining cap 3200 (4000-800)\n\nOK let me start writing code.\n\nActually let me also think about the \"follow-up contract\" semantics. The spec says:\n\"Customers receive a 15 % discount on each contract after their first.\"\n\nSo if this is the customer's Nth quote (1-indexed), they get the discount if N > 1. The \"first insurance\" surcharge is always per item, regardless.\n\nBut the spec also says first insurance applies per item. So the surcharge always applies. And follow-up discount applies if it's not the first contract.\n\nFor a single quote scenario (one quote, one customer), the customer is on their \"first contract\" so no follow-up discount. If they had a previous quote in the same scenario, then it's their second contract.\n\nSo in the integration example: \"customer: 3 years with MHPCO; this is the customer's second quote in the scenario\" → follow-up applies. The scenario has 2 quotes.\n\nLet me design the test accordingly:\n```json\n{\n  \"customer\": {\"yearsWithMHPCO\": 3},\n  \"steps\": [\n    {\"op\": \"quote\", \"items\": [{\"type\": \"sword\"}]},  // first quote, no follow-up discount\n    {\"op\": \"quote\", \"items\": [{\"type\": \"sword\", \"cursed\": true, \"enchantment\": 7}]}  // second quote, follow-up applies\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nExpected:\n- First quote: just sword with no modifiers, no follow-up → 105\n- Second quote: 100 base + 50 curse + 30 high enchant = 180, + 10 first insurance per item = 190, - 20 loyalty (20% of 100) = 170, - 15 follow-up (15% of 100) = 155, + 5 fee = 160 ✓\n\nOK good.\n\nAlright, let me now write the test list file.\n\nHmm wait, let me reconsider the test count. The spec says the measurement parses phase markers and subagent calls. I want to make sure I have enough tests but not so many that I spend forever refactoring. Let me aim for a comprehensive but manageable list.\n\nActually, the list might be 25-30 tests. That's manageable. Let me proceed.\n\nLet me write the test list file now.\n\nWait, one more thing. The spec says:\n\"Each step has an `op` field (`\"quote\"` or `\"claim\"`) and the operation-specific input fields.\"\n\nAnd the JSON schema says:\n- Quote step: requires `op`, `items`\n- Claim step: requires `op`, `policy`, `incident`\n\nSo my types should match this.\n\nAlso, for the result:\n- Quote result: requires `premium`\n- Claim result: requires `payout`, `remainingCap`\n\nOK. Let me write the test list now.\n\nActually, I realize I want to look at this once more:\n\n\"Damage to items with enchantment level ≥ 8 is reimbursed at 50 % of the damage amount.\"\n\nSo if the item in the damage report has enchantment ≥ 8, the reimbursement is 50%. But the damage report doesn't specify enchantment. It just specifies itemType and amount. So how do we know the enchantment?\n\nOH I see - we look at the item in the policy. The damage references an itemType, which we can look up in the policy.\n\nFor example, if the policy has a sword with enchantment 8, and a damage entry references \"sword\" with amount 1000, we look up the sword in the policy and see enchantment 8, apply the 50% rule.\n\nBut what if the policy has two swords, one with enchantment 8 and one with enchantment 3? And two damages each of \"sword\" with amount 1000?\n\nThe spec doesn't explicitly say. But looking at the \"two sword damages\" example: \"if the damages array contains more entries of a given type than the policy actually covers... → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code\". So 2 damages of \"sword\" against 2 swords in policy is OK. But we don't know which damage applies to which sword.\n\nI think for simplicity:\n- If multiple items of same type are in policy, and damage has same number of entries, we apply them one-to-one in order.\n- If enchantment/material varies, we still need to look at the items.\n\nHmm, but this gets complex. Let me assume for now: when a damage has itemType=\"sword\", we use the first matching sword in the policy (or any, if they have same enchantment/material).\n\nActually, let me think more carefully. The spec says \"each entry is treated as a separate damage with its own deductible\". So each damage entry is processed independently. For the enchantment/material lookup, I think we just use any matching item in the policy.\n\nFor the \"more damages than covered\" check: count damages per itemType, compare to count of items of that type in the policy.\n\nLet me not over-engineer this. I'll do:\n- For each damage entry, find a matching item in the policy (first one)\n- Apply that item's enchantment/material to determine reimbursement\n- If the policy doesn't have enough of that itemType, throw\n\nActually wait. Let me re-think. If the policy has two swords (both steel, enchant 3) and damages has two entries of sword (amount 500 each), each sword damage would use enchantment 3 → full reimbursement → 500-100=400 each → 800 total. That's clear.\n\nIf the policy has two swords with different enchantments (sword1: enchant 3, sword2: enchant 9), and damages has two entries... which one gets the high enchantment treatment? The spec doesn't say.\n\nFor this kata, I'll assume items of the same type have the same enchantment/material (i.e., the user inputs them the same). If different, the first one is used. The test list won't test this edge case.\n\nOK let me write the test list now.\n\nOne more consideration: the spec uses these examples as integration tests but doesn't give concrete numbers for all combinations. I'll only test combinations explicitly given in the spec.\n\nLet me now write the test list. I'll keep it comprehensive but organized.\n\nFinal test list structure:\n\n```typescript\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { processScenario, runCLI } from \"./claim-office.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"Quote - empty and single items\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"empty items list → premium 5 (only processing fee)\");\n  it.todo(\"single sword (no modifiers) → premium 105 (100 base + 5 fee)\");\n  it.todo(\"single amulet (no modifiers) → premium 65\");\n  it.todo(\"single staff (no modifiers) → premium 85\");\n  it.todo(\"single potion (no modifiers) → premium 45\");\n});\n\ndescribe(\"Quote - components and building blocks\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"2 runes → premium 55 (50 base + 5 fee)\");\n  it.todo(\"3 runes → premium 65 (60 base + 5 fee, block applies)\");\n  it.todo(\"4 runes → premium 105 (100 base + 5 fee, no block)\");\n  it.todo(\"7 runes → premium 180 (175 base + 5 fee)\");\n  it.todo(\"2 runes + 1 moonstone → premium 80 (75 base, no block: different types)\");\n  it.todo(\"3 runes + 3 moonstones → premium 125 (120 base, two separate blocks)\");\n});\n\ndescribe(\"Quote - item-specific modifiers\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"cursed sword: 50% surcharge on item base → premium 155\");\n  it.todo(\"sword with enchantment 5: 30% surcharge → premium 135\");\n  it.todo(\"sword with enchantment 4: no high-enchantment surcharge\");\n  it.todo(\"cursed sword with enchantment 5: both surcharges → premium 185\");\n});\n\ndescribe(\"Quote - policy-wide modifiers\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"customer with 2 years receives 20% loyalty discount\");\n  it.todo(\"customer with 1 year does NOT receive loyalty discount\");\n  it.todo(\"follow-up contract (second quote) gives 15% discount\");\n});\n\ndescribe(\"Quote - integration examples from spec\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"newcomer (0 years, first contract) with cursed sword → premium 165\");\n  it.todo(\"long-standing customer (3 years, 2nd contract) with cursed sword enchant 7 → premium 160\");\n});\n\ndescribe(\"Claim - standard reimbursement\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"regular sword (steel, enchant 3), damage 500 → payout 400 (cap 2000)\");\n  it.todo(\"rune damage 200 → payout 100 (cap 500)\");\n});\n\ndescribe(\"Claim - enchantment and dragon material clauses\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"dragon-material sword, enchant 8, damage 1000 → payout 400 (high enchantment wins, then deductible)\");\n  it.todo(\"dragon-material sword, enchant 5, damage 800 → payout 700 (dragon material only)\");\n  it.todo(\"steel sword, enchant 9, damage 1000 → payout 400 (high enchantment only)\");\n});\n\ndescribe(\"Claim - multiple damages and cap\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"dragon attack damages sword 500 + amulet 300 → payout 600 (one deductible per item)\");\n  it.todo(\"policy with two swords: insurance sum 2000, cap 4000\");\n  it.todo(\"two sword damages in one incident: each gets its own deductible\");\n  it.todo(\"first claim of 1500 on regular sword → payout 1400, remaining cap 600\");\n  it.todo(\"second claim of 1500 on regular sword → payout 600, remaining cap 0\");\n  it.todo(\"policy with sword + amulet: insurance sum 1600, cap 3200\");\n  it.todo(\"policy with sword + 3 runes (block): insurance sum 1750 (block discount affects premium only)\");\n});\n\ndescribe(\"Errors\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"quote with unknown item type throws an error\");\n  it.todo(\"claim with damage to item not in policy throws an error\");\n  it.todo(\"claim with damage to unknown item type throws an error\");\n  it.todo(\"claim with negative damage amount throws an error\");\n  it.todo(\"claim with more damages of a type than policy covers throws an error\");\n});\n\ndescribe(\"CLI behavior\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"CLI: valid scenario writes JSON results to stdout\");\n  it.todo(\"CLI: error case writes to stderr and exits non-zero\");\n});\n```\n\nThat's about 30 tests. Comprehensive but manageable.\n\nOK let me write the file now.\n\nWait, I need to think about whether tests need to import processScenario or runCLI separately. Let me design the module:\n\n`src/claim-office.ts`:\n- Exports `processScenario(scenario)` for the pure logic\n- Exports `runCLI()` for the CLI behavior (or have a separate `src/cli.ts` that uses processScenario)\n\nPer the spec, \"The CLI entry point must be at `src/cli.ts`\". So:\n- `src/claim-office.ts` — pure logic\n- `src/cli.ts` — CLI wrapper\n\nFor testing, I'll import from `./claim-office.js`.\n\nFor CLI tests, I'll spawn a subprocess. Let me have a helper in the test file:\n\n```typescript\nimport { spawnSync } from \"node:child_process\";\n\nfunction runCLI(input: string): { stdout: string; stderr: string; exitCode: number } {\n  const result = spawnSync(\"npx\", [\"tsx\", \"src/cli.ts\"], {\n    input,\n    encoding: \"utf-8\",\n  });\n  return {\n    stdout: result.stdout ?? \"\",\n    stderr: result.stderr ?? \"\",\n    exitCode: result.status ?? -1,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nActually, I think the cleanest way is to have `runCLI` exported from `claim-office.ts`. It can be a function that takes input (string or null) and returns the CLI result. Then `cli.ts` is just a thin wrapper that does process.stdin/process.stdout/exit.\n\nHmm, let me think... The simplest is to keep `claim-office.ts` as the pure logic and `cli.ts` as the CLI. Tests for the CLI behavior spawn the subprocess.\n\nActually, an even cleaner approach: make `claim-office.ts` export both `processScenario` and `runCLI`. The `runCLI` function returns the result without doing process.exit, and `cli.ts` calls it and exits accordingly.\n\nHmm, but that mixes logic. Let me think again...\n\nOK simplest approach:\n- `claim-office.ts`: exports `processScenario` and `executeCLI(args)` (the latter reads stdin, writes stdout/stderr, but doesn't exit)\n- `cli.ts`: thin wrapper that calls `executeCLI` and uses `process.exit`\n\nWait, even simpler:\n- `claim-office.ts`: exports `processScenario(scenario)` - pure\n- `cli.ts`: standalone CLI script\n\nFor CLI tests, I'll spawn the subprocess.\n\nOK let me just go with that.\n\nActually wait, I realized - for the CLI error case tests, I want to be able to test them without spawning a subprocess. But the spec specifically says \"the CLI exits with a non-zero status code and writes an error description to stderr\". So the behavior is CLI-specific.\n\nI could spawn the subprocess, but that's slow and flaky. Let me think...\n\nAlternative: my `processScenario` throws on error. My `cli.ts` catches and exits non-zero. For testing, I can:\n- Test `processScenario` throws on error (catches with `expect(...).toThrow()`)\n- Test CLI behavior by spawning subprocess\n\nOR:\n- Make a `runCLI(input)` function that returns `{stdout, stderr, exitCode}` - test this directly\n- `cli.ts` just calls `runCLI` and exits with its exitCode\n\nI'll go with the second approach. It's cleaner and faster.\n\n```typescript\n// claim-office.ts\nexport function processScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: Result[] };\n\nexport async function runCLI(input: string): Promise<{stdout: string; stderr: string; exitCode: number}> {\n  try {\n    const scenario = JSON.parse(input);\n    const result = processScenario(scenario);\n    return {\n      stdout: JSON.stringify(result),\n      stderr: \"\",\n      exitCode: 0,\n    };\n  } catch (e) {\n    return {\n      stdout: \"\",\n      stderr: e.message,\n      exitCode: 1,\n    };\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThen `cli.ts`:\n```typescript\nimport { runCLI } from \"./claim-office.js\";\n\nconst input = await readStdin();\nconst { stdout, stderr, exitCode } = await runCLI(input);\nprocess.stdout.write(stdout);\nprocess.stderr.write(stderr);\nprocess.exit(exitCode);\n```\n\nHmm, that's a clean separation. Let me go with this.\n\nActually, the simpler approach for the CLI test: use `execa` or `spawn` to spawn the actual CLI binary. Let me just do that.\n\n```typescript\nfunction runCLI(input: string): { stdout: string; stderr: string; exitCode: number } {\n  const result = spawnSync(\"node\", [\"--experimental-strip-types\", \"src/cli.ts\"], {\n    input,\n    encoding: \"utf-8\",\n  });\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nBut this requires Node 22+ with type stripping. Or use tsx.\n\nLet me just use tsx in tests:\n```typescript\nspawnSync(\"npx\", [\"tsx\", \"src/cli.ts\"], { input, encoding: \"utf-8\" })\n```\n\nOK now let me actually write the test list.\n\nOne final consideration: where do I put the implementation file? I'll have:\n- `src/claim-office.ts` - pure logic\n- `src/claim-office.spec.ts` - tests\n- `src/cli.ts` - CLI entry point\n\nOK now let me write everything.\n\nI also realized: I should be more careful about the test for \"loyalty discount threshold\". The spec says:\n\"customer with exactly 2 years with MHPCO → loyalty discount applies\"\n\nSo 2 years is the threshold. 1 year does NOT get it, 2 years does.\n\nOK now let me write the test list.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","